{
 "how": "One row per data source: the single list of what feeds the site. update.py runs each row's builder in `run.order` (skipping a row when none of its files, its builder or scripts/lib changed since the last good run; `daily` rows also run once a day). build_manifest.py writes meta-log/data/manifest.json from it (file sizes and how fresh each output is). run.find: what the row needs, each {glob: patterns in the data folder} | {data: path under the data folder} | {health: true}; all: every match, not just the newest. run.opt: the same, but optional. run.args: {0} the first find, {0*} all its files, {health} the Health export, {vitals} the extra HealthView folders, {data} the data folder. run.reads: repo files whose contents count as inputs. run.also: other rows whose files count as inputs. run.after: rows that must succeed first. fresh_by_build: true = the output's build date counts as its freshness (snapshots, exports); otherwise only real data dates count. SQL-FIRST RULE (Jack, Nov 23 2026): every NEW dataset is SQL-first: its builder writes its own store tables with scripts/lib/store.py put_table (each column tagged public, sealed or private) and never writes site JSON; the row's run.export says how scripts/export_public.py turns those tables into the dataset's day JSON (see that script). Existing JSON-first builders move to SQL-first one at a time, whenever one is next changed for another reason. sql_first: true marks a row that has moved.",
 "sources": [
  {
   "id": "health",
   "name": "Apple Health",
   "kind": "manual export",
   "raw": "jsbv data/health export YYYY-MM-DD/",
   "builder": "build_health_log.py",
   "outputs": [
    "meta-log/data/health.json"
   ],
   "pages": [
    "/#report",
    "/#explore",
    "pct-training/#recovery"
   ],
   "stale_days": 10,
   "run": {
    "order": 10,
    "label": "Apple Health",
    "find": [
     {
      "health": true
     }
    ],
    "args": [
     "{0}",
     "{vitals}"
    ]
   }
  },
